✅ WeakHashMap in Java

WeakHashMap is a special implementation of the Map interface in Java where keys are stored as weak references.

🔹 What is a Weak Reference?

Normally (like in HashMap), keys are stored with strong references → meaning the object cannot be garbage collected as long as it is in the map.
But in WeakHashMap:
Keys are stored using WeakReference
If a key has no strong reference outside the map, it becomes eligible for Garbage Collection (GC)
When GC runs → that entry is automatically removed from the map

🔹 Syntax
Map<KeyType, ValueType> map = new WeakHashMap<>();


🔹 Important Points
Feature	WeakHashMap
Order maintained?	❌ No
Allows null key?	✅ Yes (1 null key)
Thread safe?	    ❌ No
Reference type  	Weak reference (for keys only)
GC removes entry?	✅ Yes

🔹 Where is it Used?

✔ Caching
✔ Storing metadata
✔ Listener registration
✔ Memory-sensitive mappings

Example: If you want objects to disappear automatically when no longer used.

🔹 Difference: HashMap vs WeakHashMap
HashMap     	               WeakHashMap
Strongreference	                Weak reference
Entry stays until removed   	Entry removed by GC
Manual removal required     	Automatic removal

If interviewer asks 👇
Q: Why use WeakHashMap?
👉 To avoid memory leaks when keys should not prevent garbage collection.